{
  "v638_observed_scan": "How to read the figure. The upper panel compares observed 90% profile-CLs limits under three assumed signal models. Each yield refers to its full signal distribution, so the Gaussian curves are not limits on the same MC-shaped signal as the neighboring-template curve. The lower panel shows fixed-mass asymptotic excess p-values; these are not globally calibrated probabilities. Points identify the three nonoverlapping selected excess windows and a deficit window, whose locations were chosen using these data. The shaded 60\u201380 MeV interval crosses the detector acceptance turn-on and has no independent intermediate-mass MC validation. Lines have no uncertainty bands; fixed signal-template and GP inputs do not propagate model or selection uncertainty.",
  "v638_peak_1": "How to read the figure. Black points show observed counts divided by the bin width, with sqrt(N) count-only errors. The blue curve is the GP sideband prediction, and its band is one marginal standard deviation of the background constraint; it is not a post-fit confidence band. Gold is the profiled background-only fit. Green is the background component of the signed signal-plus-background fit, and red is its total. In the lower panel the same GP mean is subtracted from data and backgrounds; purple isolates the signed signal contribution. All profiled curves are restricted to the likelihood bins, which are excluded from GP training. The quoted yield is full-selected and signed, with a conditional Hessian error. This hypothesis lies in the acceptance-transition interpolation between 60 and 80 MeV, without independent intermediate-mass signal MC. The fitted positive signal is an excess; the displayed probabilities are fixed-mass local references. The region was selected using the same observed scan; neither the asymptotic nor toy probability includes the mass-search selection. Toy rank probabilities use the add-one rule, while the stated exact two-sided 95% Clopper\u2013Pearson interval describes the underlying exceedance fraction. Disjoint fitted intervals still share GP sidebands and are not independent measurements.",
